::CW_U06_UA_Q01::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q01
Which action best begins integrated portfolio preparation?{
~A. Decorate the portfolio before checking contents.#Incorrect. First check completeness and purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Submit the first file found.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. List required parts and mark each present, missing, or needing revision.#Correct. This gives a clear readiness map. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Remove the author statement.#Incorrect. Reflection is part of portfolio synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q02::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q02
Which piece belongs in a final portfolio?{
=A. A revised piece that shows a specific craft skill and reader effect.#Correct. This supports portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A piece with no title and no revision.#Incorrect. This is not reader-ready. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A favorite piece the writer cannot explain.#Incorrect. Preference alone is weak evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A draft with comments still visible.#Incorrect. Draft comments weaken final readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q03::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q03
A portfolio statement discusses voice. Which evidence best matches it?{
~A. A random image pasted beside the title.#Incorrect. This does not show voice development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A revised story excerpt where dialogue and sentence rhythm reveal character voice.#Correct. This directly supports the stated craft growth.
~C. A file name with no writing attached.#Incorrect. A file name is not craft evidence.
~D. A paragraph saying the writer worked hard.#Incorrect. Effort alone does not show voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q04::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q04
Which portfolio order has the clearest purpose?{
~A. Alphabetical by file name with no explanation.#Incorrect. This may not guide craft understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Random order based on download time.#Incorrect. Random order does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Longest to shortest only because length matters most.#Incorrect. Length is not the main purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Story first for revised dialogue, poem second for voice through rhythm, statement last to explain growth.#Correct. This order guides the reader through evidence.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q05::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q05
Which note is most useful during a readiness pass?{
=A. Preview script and make character labels consistent.#Correct. This names a specific publication-readiness action.
~B. Fix it later.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Maybe change something.#Incorrect. This does not identify the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It looks fine from far away.#Incorrect. Readiness requires specific checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q06::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q06
How should an author statement connect to selected pieces?{
~A. It should discuss unrelated goals only.#Incorrect. The statement should connect to the work.
~B. It should hide all revision evidence.#Incorrect. Revision evidence supports growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It should name skills the reader can see in the portfolio.#Correct. The statement and evidence should support each other.
~D. It should replace the creative pieces.#Incorrect. It explains the pieces; it does not replace them.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q07::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q07
Which presentation choice best supports the portfolio?{
~A. Read the longest excerpt regardless of purpose.#Incorrect. Excerpt choice should be focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Choose one excerpt that shows a craft choice connected to portfolio growth.#Correct. This connects presentation and portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Avoid explaining the excerpt.#Incorrect. The presentation needs craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Summarize every assignment from the course.#Incorrect. That is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q08::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q08
Which problem should be fixed before final publication?{
~A. The writer can explain the piece clearly.#Incorrect. That is a strength, not a problem.
~B. The portfolio has a clear title.#Incorrect. A clear title supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The author statement names revision growth.#Incorrect. That supports synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The final file includes bracketed notes that say add ending later.#Correct. Draft notes show the piece is not final.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q09::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q09
What makes a portfolio integrated?{
=A. The selected pieces, statement, revision evidence, and presentation plan connect to shared growth.#Correct. Integration means the parts work together. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. The files are unrelated but colorful.#Incorrect. Visual decoration does not create integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The portfolio has no explanation.#Incorrect. Author reasoning is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The presentation ignores the pieces.#Incorrect. The presentation should connect to the portfolio.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q10::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q10
Which answer best explains reader effect?{
~A. I put this first because I found it first.#Incorrect. This does not explain reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I revised the line breaks so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a choice and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I added random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I like the color.#Incorrect. Preference is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q11::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q11
A student marks "needs revision" beside the presentation plan. What should come next?{
~A. Ignore the note.#Incorrect. The issue should be resolved. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Delete the portfolio.#Incorrect. One issue does not require deleting everything.
=C. Write the exact missing action, such as add listening focus.#Correct. Specific actions guide revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Submit without checking.#Incorrect. Readiness requires follow-through. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q12::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q12
Which choice shows revision evidence?{
=A. A draft and final version with a note explaining how feedback improved dialogue.#Correct. This shows change and reasoning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A blank page.#Incorrect. There is no evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A final piece with no explanation and no draft trail.#Incorrect. This may not show revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A decoration folder.#Incorrect. Decoration is not revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q13::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q13
Which author rationale is strongest?{
~A. I included this because it was easy.#Incorrect. Ease is not a strong rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I included this because it is first alphabetically.#Incorrect. File order is not craft rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I included this because I needed another page.#Incorrect. Filler is not portfolio evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I included this poem because revising the image pattern made the speaker's change clearer.#Correct. This connects selection, revision, and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q14::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q14
Which statement describes publication formatting?{
~A. Changing the genre completely.#Incorrect. Formatting prepares the genre; it does not automatically change it.
=B. Preparing the piece with title, spacing, labels, conventions, and file readiness.#Correct. These are reader-facing format concerns. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Avoiding all revision.#Incorrect. Formatting and revision can both matter. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Refusing to preview the file.#Incorrect. Previewing supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q15::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q15
What should a student do if the portfolio order feels random?{
~A. Leave it random.#Incorrect. Random order weakens integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove every piece.#Incorrect. Reordering is the better fix. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Create a reader-focused order and explain the reason.#Correct. Purposeful order helps the reader follow the portfolio.
~D. Add unrelated colors.#Incorrect. Colors do not solve organization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q16::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q16
Which checkpoint response shows mastery?{
=A. My portfolio order moves from revised story to poem to statement so readers see voice growth across genres.#Correct. This explains order and craft growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I am done because I clicked submit.#Incorrect. Submission alone does not show mastery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I chose whatever was easiest.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I cannot explain the order.#Incorrect. Mastery includes rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q17::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q17
Which final presentation note is strongest?{
~A. Listen to everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I do not know what to listen for.#Incorrect. The presenter needs focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignore the excerpt.#Incorrect. The excerpt is central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Listen for how the clipped dialogue creates tension after the narrator avoids the question.#Correct. This gives a clear listening focus. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q18::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q18
Which issue is a portfolio completeness problem?{
~A. The title is clear.#Incorrect. That supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The required author statement is missing.#Correct. A missing required part must be resolved.
~C. The presentation has a listening focus.#Incorrect. That is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The revised piece has paragraph breaks.#Incorrect. That supports readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q19::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q19
Why should the student avoid vague notes like "fix portfolio"?{
~A. They are too detailed.#Incorrect. They are not detailed enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. They replace the need for revision.#Incorrect. They do not guide revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. They do not identify the exact action needed.#Correct. Specific notes make revision possible. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. They prove mastery.#Incorrect. Mastery requires clear action and reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q20::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q20
Which answer best connects publication and presentation?{
=A. The portfolio shows the final work, and the presentation explains how selected craft choices work.#Correct. This captures the relationship between product and author reasoning.
~B. The presentation should ignore the portfolio.#Incorrect. They should connect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Publication means no one reads the piece.#Incorrect. Publication prepares work for readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Presentation means only summary.#Incorrect. Presentation includes craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q21::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q21
Which choice should be removed from a final portfolio file?{
~A. Clear title.#Incorrect. A clear title should remain. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Unresolved comment saying check spelling.#Correct. Unresolved comments make the file look unfinished.
~C. Intentional line breaks.#Incorrect. Purposeful line breaks support poetry. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Author name if required.#Incorrect. Required author information should remain. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q22::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q22
Which answer is strongest when explaining a selected excerpt?{
~A. I selected it randomly.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I selected it because it is confusing.#Incorrect. Confusion does not support audience understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I selected it because it shows the exact revision I describe in my statement.#Correct. This connects excerpt and author reflection. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I selected it but will not explain it.#Incorrect. Presentation requires explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q23::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q23
Which readiness pass checks genre-specific format?{
~A. Make every genre look identical.#Incorrect. Genres have different format needs. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all titles.#Incorrect. Titles often prepare readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing distracts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Check poems for intentional line breaks and scripts for clear character labels.#Correct. Genre-specific formatting supports readers. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q24::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q24
A student says the statement and portfolio do not match. What is the best fix?{
=A. Align the statement's claims with evidence in the selected pieces.#Correct. This creates a coherent portfolio. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Hide the selected pieces.#Incorrect. The portfolio needs evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Delete all reflection.#Incorrect. Reflection is part of the course. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Ignore the mismatch.#Incorrect. Mismatch weakens integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q25::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q25
Which final decision best shows Unit 6 integration?{
~A. Submit one rough draft only.#Incorrect. That is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Submit a presentation with no portfolio.#Incorrect. The unit requires integrated work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Submit unrelated files without explanation.#Incorrect. Integration requires purpose and connection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Submit publication-ready pieces, a connected author statement, revision evidence, and a presentation plan with craft focus.#Correct. This combines the unit's core skills.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q26::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q26
Portfolio context: A writer says, "I chose this poem because I like it." What is the best revision of the selection reason?{
~A. I chose this poem because it was short.#Incorrect. Length alone is not craft evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I chose this poem because I found it first.#Incorrect. File order does not prove portfolio value.
=C. I chose this poem because the revised final image shows stronger control of imagery and emotion.#Correct. This gives a craft-based reason supported by revision evidence.
~D. I chose no pieces because portfolios do not need writing.#Incorrect. A portfolio requires selected work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q27::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q27
Which item is the strongest evidence for a craft rationale?{
~A. A statement that the piece exists.#Incorrect. Existence is not evidence of craft growth.
=B. A before-and-after revision that changes "I was lonely" into a specific bus stop image.#Correct. This shows a concrete revision and craft improvement.
~C. A random font change.#Incorrect. Random formatting does not show craft rationale.
~D. A sentence saying the piece is good.#Incorrect. This is vague praise, not evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q28::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.REV.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q28
Which three parts belong in a complete craft rationale?{
=A. Selection claim, evidence, and reader effect.#Correct. These parts explain why the piece belongs in the portfolio.
~B. Title, font, and file name.#Incorrect. These do not explain craft growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Summary, summary, and more summary.#Incorrect. A rationale needs evidence and effect, not only summary.
~D. Favorite line, favorite color, and page number.#Incorrect. These do not form a complete rationale.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q29::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q29
Author statement: "I guess this poem is fine, and people can think whatever." What is the main tone problem?{
~A. It is too specific and reflective.#Incorrect. The statement is not specific or reflective.
~B. It explains too much craft reasoning.#Incorrect. It gives almost no craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It sounds uncertain and dismissive.#Correct. The tone does not fit a publication-ready author statement.
~D. It is too organized.#Incorrect. The statement lacks clear organization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q30::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q30
Which author statement sentence has the strongest tone?{
~A. This is good and nobody should question it.#Incorrect. This sounds defensive and exaggerated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I wrote stuff and it happened.#Incorrect. This is vague and careless. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. People probably will not get it.#Incorrect. This sounds dismissive rather than reflective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I revised this piece to make the speaker's uncertainty clearer through repeated images.#Correct. The tone is reflective, specific, and publication-ready.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q31::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q31
Story problem: Readers do not understand why Ellis leaves at the end. Which revision best fits?{
~A. Change Ellis's shirt color.#Incorrect. This does not clarify motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Add one action showing Ellis choosing to leave before the final line.#Correct. This directly clarifies motivation and sequence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Add random weather description.#Incorrect. Weather does not automatically solve character motivation.
~D. Delete every character.#Incorrect. This removes the story instead of revising it.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q32::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.06
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q32
Story problem: A suspense scene moves too quickly. Which revision best improves pacing?{
=A. Add action beats as the character listens at the door and watches the light shift.#Correct. Action beats slow the moment and build suspense.
~B. Remove the entire scene.#Incorrect. The scene can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add a random joke.#Incorrect. That may weaken suspense. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Change every name.#Incorrect. Names do not control pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q33::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q33
Poem problem: The line says, "I felt sad." Feedback asks for a stronger image. Which revision best fits?{
~A. I felt very sad and sadder.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Sadness is a feeling.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Rain gathered on the sill until the glass forgot the street.#Correct. This uses a concrete image to suggest sadness.
~D. I deleted the poem.#Incorrect. The poem can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q34::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.POE.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q34
Poem problem: The final choice happens too quickly. Which revision best uses line breaks?{
~A. Add random breaks after every letter.#Incorrect. Random breaks distract readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Place the final phrase on its own line so the reader pauses before the decision.#Correct. This uses line breaks to control pacing.
~C. Remove all line breaks forever.#Incorrect. Line breaks can support meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Change the poem into a grocery list.#Incorrect. This does not address pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q35::
[MLA.CW.COM.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q35
A student is preparing a poem for digital publication. Which formatting choice best supports the genre?{
~A. Put the entire poem in one paragraph so it uses less space.#Incorrect. A poem usually needs intentional line breaks and stanza breaks so the reader can follow image, sound, and pacing.
~B. Add random symbols before every line.#Incorrect. Decoration can distract readers if it does not support meaning.
~C. Remove the title because poems do not need one.#Incorrect. A title often prepares the reader for the poem's focus or tone.
=D. Keep the title clear and preserve purposeful line and stanza breaks.#Correct. This matches the poem's genre and helps the reader see how the poem moves.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q36::
[MLA.CW.PUB.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q36
A short story draft appears as one long block of text with dialogue inside the same paragraph. What should the writer do first?{
=A. Add paragraph breaks when the speaker changes and when the action shifts.#Correct. Paragraphing helps readers follow dialogue, action, and pacing in prose.
~B. Change every sentence to a different font.#Incorrect. Font changes do not solve the readability problem.
~C. Remove all dialogue marks.#Incorrect. This would make the dialogue harder to follow.
~D. Add extra blank lines after every sentence.#Incorrect. Random spacing can make the piece look unfinished.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q37::
[MLA.CW.WR.08] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q37
An author presentation begins with a short story excerpt. Which opening best prepares the audience?{
~A. I am reading something I wrote, and that is all you need to know.#Incorrect. The audience needs title, genre, and a useful frame.
=B. My piece is a short story called The Last Bus. Listen for the short sentences because they show panic.#Correct. This gives title, genre, brief context, and a listening focus.
~C. This is long, so I will explain every event before reading.#Incorrect. Too much summary can overwhelm the presentation.
~D. I picked this because it is my favorite.#Incorrect. Personal preference alone does not prepare the audience.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q38::
[MLA.CW.COM.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q38
Which excerpt choice is strongest for an author presentation?{
~A. The longest section, even if it needs ten minutes of explanation.#Incorrect. Long excerpts can make the presentation unfocused.
~B. A random middle section with no context.#Incorrect. The audience needs a clear entry point.
=C. A short section that clearly shows a craft choice and can be understood with brief context.#Correct. A focused excerpt helps the audience notice the writer's craft.
~D. The first page only because first pages are always best.#Incorrect. The best excerpt depends on the presentation purpose.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q39::
[MLA.CW.REV.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q39
Which portfolio choice is strongest?{
~A. Choose the longest piece only because it fills space.#Incorrect. Length alone does not prove portfolio value.
~B. Choose the favorite piece with no explanation.#Incorrect. A portfolio choice needs evidence and rationale.
~C. Choose a piece with unresolved draft comments.#Incorrect. Final portfolio work should be reader-ready.
=D. Choose a revised piece that shows clear craft growth and can be explained.#Correct. This connects selection, revision, and author reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_UA_Q40::
[MLA.CW.PUB.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U06_UA_Q40
A student notices the author statement discusses imagery, but no selected piece shows imagery. What should the student do?{
=A. Revise the statement or portfolio so the evidence and explanation match.#Correct. The statement and portfolio should support each other.
~B. Leave the mismatch because readers will guess.#Incorrect. Readers should not have to guess the connection.
~C. Remove all craft language from the statement.#Incorrect. Craft language is needed for author reasoning.
~D. Add random images to every page.#Incorrect. Random additions do not create purposeful evidence.
}
